# CeraLive — Canonical SHARED runtime package list
#
# Arch-independent Debian packages every CeraLive device installs, regardless of
# SoC/board. This is the SINGLE source of truth, reconciled from three diverging
# legacy sources (Task 18):
#   1. configs/base/ceraui-base.conf        (BASE_PACKAGES + STREAMING_PACKAGES)
#   2. userpatches/customize-image.sh        (the set actually installed in v1)
#   3. configs/devices/{rock5bplus,orangepi5plus}.conf (BOARD_PACKAGES — BSP only)
#
# NOT here (by design, see removed.md for the full accounting):
#   - BSP / HW-accel (kernel, DTB, U-Boot, firmware, gstreamer1.0-rockchip1,
#     rockchip-multimedia-config) → manifests/families/rk3588.yaml
#   - RK3588-specific runtime deltas → rk3588.delta.list (currently empty)
#   - First-party .debs (the CERALIVE/srt runtime, cerastream, srtla-send-rs, and
#     CeraUI) → installed in the APPLICATION layer (Stage 3), never fetched from
#     Debian by this manifest. The SRT package replaces Debian's TLS-flavor
#     variants in the same offline local transaction; see LAYER-MAP.md §libsrt.
#   - Debug/diagnostic tooling (python3, strace, tcpdump, htop, lsof, …) →
#     development.delta.list, resolved ONLY when CERALIVE_DEBUG_IMAGE=1
#   - On-device build tooling (build-essential, git, cmake, gdb, nodejs, …) →
#     NOT shipped at all, in either variant; see removed.md §(e)
#   - openssh-server, systemd, udev, dbus → already in the mkosi base layer
#
# Format: one package per line; `#` comments justify non-obvious entries.
# The mkosi runtime layer (mkosi/mkosi.images/runtime/mkosi.conf) installs
# exactly this set; lib/parity-check.sh diffs the built rootfs against it.

# --- TLS / apt plumbing -------------------------------------------------------
ca-certificates          # TLS trust store; required for https apt + apt.ceralive.tv mTLS
# apt-transport-https    # REMOVED (todo 19, audited; re-verified against trixie in
                         # todo 10). On trixie this is STILL a `Section: oldlibs`
                         # TRANSITIONAL DUMMY (3.0.3) whose own Debian description reads
                         # "transitional package for https support" — https support moved
                         # into apt itself in 1.5. It ships no apt method and no code at
                         # all, and trixie's apt is 3.0.3, far past the 1.5 cutover.
                         # The device's own https+mTLS apt source (apt.ceralive.tv, see the
                         # `_apt`-readable client-key KEY FACT in AGENTS.md) is served by
                         # apt's built-in https method, not by this package.
openssl                  # CLI for the first-boot self-signed TLS cert (ceralive-tls-firstboot).
                         # Priority: important on Debian (usually already present), pinned
                         # explicitly so cert generation never depends on a transitive pull.

# --- CeraUI TLS front (Task 15, SC3) ------------------------------------------
nginx-light              # terminates HTTPS on 443 and reverse-proxies to the CeraUI
                         # backend on 127.0.0.1:80 (WebSocket-upgrade aware, EC6). Port 80
                         # is LEFT to the backend (no redirect). nginx-light is the smallest
                         # viable nginx flavour — it carries the proxy + ssl modules without
                         # the mail/stream/extras bulk of nginx-full. Cert is per-device
                         # self-signed on /data; see docs/size-notes.md for the size delta.

# --- Network management -------------------------------------------------------
network-manager          # primary connection manager (WiFi/Ethernet/modem); CeraUI drives it
systemd-resolved         # DNS resolver; NetworkManager uses dns=systemd-resolved (postinst-lib.sh). On Debian trixie this is still a SEPARATE package (257.13-1~deb13u1) — it was bundled in systemd on v1's Armbian base, so it must stay named explicitly.
dnsmasq                  # DHCP/DNS for hostapd AP mode + local resolution
iproute2                 # `ip` — CeraUI route/policy-route read-only diagnostics
net-tools                # ships /sbin/ifconfig — CeraUI's backend polls it every ~5s
                         # (apps/backend network-interfaces.ts run("ifconfig", [])) to build
                         # the `netif` broadcast (WiFi/Ethernet/cellular/bonded-link status).
                         # This minimal trixie image ships only modern iproute2, so WITHOUT
                         # net-tools every poll tick fails ("Executable not found in $PATH:
                         # \"ifconfig\"") and the Network destination renders completely empty
                         # ("No WiFi/wired interfaces", "No SIM cards", "No active links") plus a
                         # missing Ethernet entry in Bonded Links, despite live, connected NICs
                         # (confirmed on real Rock 5B+ hardware). CeraUI's ifconfig text-parsing is
                         # deeply embedded in its test suite; adding the one legacy binary is the
                         # minimal-risk fix, NOT rewriting network-interfaces.ts onto `ip`.
ipcalc                   # subnet math in the NM dispatcher (customize-image.sh:358)

# --- USB modem bring-up -------------------------------------------------------
usb-modeswitch           # flips USB modems out of storage mode into modem mode —
                         # required for USB LTE/5G modem enumeration (core datapath).
                         # NOTE: audio (alsa-utils/pulseaudio) and the rest of the USB/HW
                         # introspection set (usbutils/pciutils/lsof/i2c-tools/can-utils)
                         # moved to the debug add-on (T17) — see removed.md.
uhubctl                  # per-port USB power cycling (trixie main 2.6.0-1, ~100 KB). A wedged
                         # LTE/5G dongle that stops answering its control port cannot be
                         # recovered by any software reset — the datapath fix is a physical
                         # replug, and on a headless board mounted in a rack there is nobody
                         # to perform one. uhubctl drives the hub's PORT_POWER feature so the
                         # device re-enumerates exactly as a replug would.
                         # SCOPE: this entry ships the BINARY only. modem-stack exposes
                         # uhubctl strictly as an INJECTED port with no default
                         # implementation and it is NOT in its shipped provider set, so
                         # nothing on the image power-cycles a port on its own. Do not add a
                         # unit, udev rule or timer that invokes it as a side effect —
                         # cutting power to a hub port during a live stream drops the bond
                         # link it carries. Whether a given hub honours per-port switching is
                         # a hardware fact (`uhubctl` reports it), never assumed here.

# --- GStreamer core (streaming pipeline; HW-accel plugin is in the family manifest)
gstreamer1.0-tools       # gst-launch/gst-inspect — pipeline plumbing
gstreamer1.0-alsa        # alsasrc — USB audio capture for audio-capable streaming pipelines
gstreamer1.0-nice         # nicesrc — WebRTC ICE transport for peer connectivity
gstreamer1.0-plugins-base
gstreamer1.0-plugins-good
gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad
gstreamer1.0-plugins-ugly
gstreamer1.0-libav       # libav-backed encode/decode fallback
v4l-utils                # v4l2-ctl + media-ctl for HDMI/UVC capture device control
ffmpeg                   # transcode/probe utility for the pipeline

# --- Modems (LTE/5G on every board) -------------------------------------------
# These pull the FULL Debian modem dependency tree (libmm-glib0, libqmi-glib5,
# libmbim-glib4, libqrtr-glib0, plus external deps: glib, libgudev, polkit, …) in
# the runtime layer. The APP layer then UPGRADES the nine ceralive-forked ELF
# packages (the ModemManager 1.24 closure staged from apt.ceralive.tv) over these
# Debian versions in one local dpkg transaction — see mkosi.images/app/
# mkosi.postinst.chroot RUNTIME_APP_PKGS and docs/modem-matrix.md §1.
modemmanager             # cellular modem management daemon
libqmi-utils             # QMI modem control (Qualcomm)
libmbim-utils            # MBIM modem control
mobile-broadband-provider-info  # ModemManager's mobile-provider / APN database
                         # (/usr/share/mobile-broadband-provider-info). Only a
                         # ModemManager `Recommends:`, so --no-install-recommends
                         # never pulls it transitively (same trap as wireless-regdb).
                         # Absent it, ModemManager cannot auto-resolve APN/provider
                         # settings from the operator's MCCMNC and every SIM needs
                         # a manual connection profile. Debian package (not forked).

# --- WiFi ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# wireless-tools         # REMOVED (todo 19, audited). Legacy WEXT tooling — it ships ONLY
                         # iwconfig/iwlist/iwgetid/iwpriv/iwspy/iwevent, and NOTHING on this
                         # device can invoke any of them. CeraUI's backend may execute only
                         # the binaries in its security-reviewed allowlist
                         # (apps/backend/src/helpers/run.ts `ALLOWED`), which carries `iw`
                         # and NO WEXT binary, so the WEXT path is structurally unreachable
                         # rather than merely unused. WiFi association is NetworkManager +
                         # wpa_supplicant over D-Bus, and regulatory/channel work is
                         # `iw reg get/set` + `iw phy` (modules/wifi/regdomain.ts) — the `iw`
                         # entry immediately below is the package that provides it. cerastream
                         # never touches WiFi, no shipped unit or script under mkosi/ names
                         # a WEXT binary, and no installed package Depends/Recommends it. The
                         # only iwconfig/iwlist references anywhere are prose and CeraUI's
                         # host-side test-harness/wifi-hwsim container (mac80211_hwsim on a
                         # dev box), never the device.
iw                       # nl80211 tool: `iw reg get/set` applies the operator's regulatory
                         # country, and `iw phy` is what CeraUI reads BACK to derive the
                         # AP-usable hotspot channels (no country->channel table exists
                         # anywhere). wireless-tools does NOT provide it — that package ships
                         # only the legacy WEXT iwconfig/iwlist/iwgetid binaries — and nothing
                         # else in this list pulls it, so without an explicit entry the
                         # regulatory country cannot be applied and the hotspot stays on the
                         # conservative world domain.
wpasupplicant            # WPA client
wireless-regdb           # wireless regulatory DB (/lib/firmware/regulatory.db + .p7s) — the
                         # kernel cfg80211 subsystem loads it at boot to establish a usable
                         # regulatory domain. It is wpasupplicant's `Recommends:`, so it is
                         # NOT pulled transitively (this list installs with
                         # --no-install-recommends); named explicitly or cfg80211 boots with
                         # "Direct firmware load for regulatory.db failed with error -2" and
                         # NetworkManager finds no usable WiFi interface (real-HW UART, 2026-07-16).
hostapd                  # AP mode (device hotspot for config)

# --- Bluetooth ----------------------------------------------------------------
bluez                    # the Bluetooth userspace stack: bluetoothd + bluetoothctl/btmgmt.
                         # The KERNEL half already works and always did — on a Rock 5B+ the
                         # RTL8852BE's Bluetooth radio enumerates as USB 13d3:3572, btusb+btrtl
                         # bind it, and /sys/class/bluetooth/hci0 exists (board-confirmed on
                         # 7.1.5, 2026-08-02). What was missing is entirely userspace: with no
                         # bluez there is no bluetoothd, no bluetooth.service ("Unit
                         # bluetooth.service could not be found"), and no bluetoothctl/hciconfig,
                         # so the adapter can never be powered up, paired or used. libbluetooth3
                         # was present only as a transitive dependency of an unrelated package,
                         # which is why `dpkg -l | grep blue` looked deceptively non-empty.
                         # This is NOT an edge-kernel regression — the vendor-BSP images have the
                         # same gap. Same class as the net-tools / iw entries above: one missing
                         # userspace package silently disables a whole hardware feature.
# bluez-alsa-utils       # REMOVED (todo 28). SUPERSEDED, not dropped: PipeWire's own
# libasound2-plugin-bluez #   BlueZ backend (libspa-0.2-bluetooth, below) is now the
                         #   device's Bluetooth-audio provider, and the two are
                         #   MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by
                         #   preference. BlueZ permits exactly ONE service to register
                         #   as the provider of a given Bluetooth audio profile, so a
                         #   second one simply loses; bluez-alsa's own TROUBLESHOOTING
                         #   §6 says running BlueALSA alongside PipeWire with its
                         #   Bluetooth modules enabled "is not advisable", and the
                         #   observable failure is WirePlumber logging
                         #   "Properties changed in unknown transport …" plus an
                         #   explicit multiple-sound-server warning.
                         #   cerastream ADR-0010 "BT manager exclusivity" states the
                         #   rule and hands enforcement to THIS repository, because the
                         #   engine has no Bluetooth vocabulary at all: it receives an
                         #   opaque PCM or node address and nothing more.
                         #   THERE IS NO SUPPORTED "BOTH INSTALLED, ONE DISABLED"
                         #   CONFIGURATION — a masked-unit arrangement is one apt
                         #   upgrade away from unmasking itself — so the packages are
                         #   removed rather than left present and suppressed.
                         #   The ALSA rollback hatch is unaffected: `[audio] backend =
                         #   "alsa"` still reaches a physical `hw:CARD=` capture card
                         #   through `alsasrc` (gstreamer1.0-alsa, unchanged). What the
                         #   ALSA arm loses with these two packages is the
                         #   `bluealsa:DEV=…,PROFILE=sco` opaque-PCM route to a
                         #   BLUETOOTH microphone specifically — that hardware is
                         #   reached on the PipeWire arm instead, as an ordinary node.

# --- Audio server (PipeWire, system mode — ADR-0010) --------------------------
# The device shares ONE capture card between the always-on audio meter sidecar and
# the live program-audio leg. ALSA hands out exactly one capture handle per card, so
# under `[audio] backend = "alsa"` the engine has to arbitrate them with the ADR-0007
# ownership lease — seven states, four bounded timers, acknowledged handoffs in both
# directions, and an amendment history of real field defects (0/5 and 2/5 launches
# failing `audio-device-unavailable` until a whole-launch card reservation was added).
# PipeWire multiplexes the device instead, so both consumers hold it at once and the
# lease stops being on the hot path. See cerastream docs/adr/ADR-0010-pipewire-capture.md.
#
# TRIXIE-NATIVE VERSIONS, resolved against the REAL trixie arm64 index (todo 28) —
# NOT a forecast, and one of them is NOT what the version-family guess would give:
#   pipewire              1.4.2-1
#   pipewire-alsa         1.4.2-1
#   gstreamer1.0-pipewire 1.4.2-1
#   libspa-0.2-bluetooth  1.4.2-1
#   wireplumber           0.5.8-2   <-- session manager versions INDEPENDENTLY of
#                                       PipeWire; assuming 1.4.2 here would pin a
#                                       version that does not exist. Same class of
#                                       trap as todo 10's libwpewebkit rename.
# `libpipewire-0.3-0` does NOT exist on trixie either — the t64 transition renamed it
# `libpipewire-0.3-0t64`. Nothing below names it directly (it arrives transitively),
# which is exactly why the name is recorded here rather than discovered at a flip.
pipewire                 # the daemon itself. Debian ships it with USER units ONLY
                         # (/usr/lib/systemd/user/{pipewire.service,pipewire.socket}) —
                         # there is NO system unit to enable and NO system unit to drop
                         # in over, verified by `dpkg-deb -c` on the real trixie arm64
                         # package. This is a headless appliance with no login session,
                         # no seat and no XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, so the SYSTEM units are
                         # first-party and shipped from mkosi/runtime/pipewire/ by
                         # postinst-lib.sh::setup_pipewire_system_mode.
                         # Its postinst creates the `pipewire` system GROUP (for the
                         # packaged /etc/security/limits.d/25-pw-rlimits.conf); the
                         # matching system USER is ours to create, and those PAM limits
                         # are INERT for a systemd service — the unit's own LimitRTPRIO=
                         # / LimitRTTIME= / LimitMEMLOCK= are what actually apply.
wireplumber              # the session manager. Upstream is unambiguous that starting a
                         # session manager from pipewire.conf's `context.exec` "has been
                         # demoted to development aid" and must be avoided in production
                         # (pipewire.conf(5)), so it gets its own system unit. Also
                         # USER-units-only in Debian. Its headless profile — no session
                         # bus, no logind seat, no device reservation — is a first-party
                         # wireplumber.conf.d fragment.
pipewire-alsa            # /usr/share/alsa/alsa.conf.d/50-pipewire.conf: routes the ALSA
                         # DEFAULT pcm through PipeWire so an ALSA-only client is not
                         # locked out of a card the daemon holds. It does NOT intercept
                         # an explicit `hw:CARD=<id>` address, which is the exact
                         # vocabulary the ALSA rollback hatch uses, so `backend = "alsa"`
                         # keeps opening the card directly.
                         # CARRIES A HARD `Conflicts: pulseaudio` — that is why
                         # `pulseaudio` had to leave development.delta.list; see the
                         # removal note there. A coinstall was attempted against the real
                         # index and REFUSED ("Reached two conflicting decisions").
gstreamer1.0-pipewire    # `pipewiresrc` — the element cerastream builds its capture
                         # source from on the PipeWire arm (`pipewiresrc
                         # target-object=<node>`). Without it a PipeWire-configured graph
                         # fails construction with the typed
                         # GraphError::AudioBackendUnavailable and NEVER silently falls
                         # back to ALSA, and the engine withholds its `pipewire-capture`
                         # feature token so CeraUI gates closed. The engine `.deb`
                         # deliberately does NOT Depends on this (the default arm does not
                         # need it), so the image is the only thing that can supply it.
libspa-0.2-bluetooth     # PipeWire's BlueZ backend — the replacement for bluez-alsa
                         # above. A Bluetooth microphone becomes an ordinary PipeWire
                         # node with a `node.name` and no `api.alsa.*` property, which
                         # cerastream's `sources/pw_identity.rs` resolves through the
                         # SAME resolver as a physical card. Not a `Recommends:` of
                         # anything we install, so it must be named explicitly or BT
                         # audio is silently absent.

# --- mDNS / discovery ---------------------------------------------------------
avahi-daemon             # .local hostname advertisement (find the box on the LAN)
avahi-utils              # set/query Avahi's published hostname during allocation + health checks
libnss-mdns              # NSS plugin so the box can resolve other .local hosts

# --- Time ---------------------------------------------------------------------
chrony                   # NTP client; enabled service in customize-image.sh:521.
                         # NOTE: v1 enabled the unit but never apt-installed it — fixed here.

# --- CPU / power --------------------------------------------------------------
linux-cpupower           # CPU governor control for sustained encode performance. RENAMED
                         # AT THE TRIXIE MIGRATION (todo 10): the previous entry was
                         # `cpufrequtils`, which Debian REMOVED — it left testing on
                         # 2023-10-28 and unstable on 2024-06-16, so it exists in bookworm  # suite-literal-ok: names the suite the removed package still exists in, which is why the rename was needed
                         # and resolves to NOTHING on trixie (verified against the real
                         # trixie arm64 index: no candidate in main/contrib/non-free). An
                         # unmigrated entry fails the runtime layer outright with
                         # `E: Unable to locate package cpufrequtils`.
                         #
                         # THE REPLACEMENT IS NOT DROP-IN, AND THAT IS THE TRAP. Swapping
                         # the package name alone SILENTLY LOSES the governor pin, because
                         # the two packages deliver it by completely different means:
                         #   * cpufrequtils shipped `/etc/init.d/cpufrequtils` +
                         #     `S01cpufrequtils`/`S01loadcpufreq` sysv links, which read
                         #     `GOVERNOR=` out of `/etc/default/cpufrequtils` at boot.
                         #     That config file is what the runtime postinst wrote.
                         #   * linux-cpupower (6.12.105-1, built from the Debian `linux`
                         #     SOURCE package) ships EXACTLY ONE file — `/usr/bin/cpupower`.
                         #     No unit, no init script, no `/etc/default` hook. Verified by
                         #     `dpkg -L` in a real trixie arm64 container. Nothing anywhere
                         #     in trixie reads `/etc/default/cpufrequtils` any more, so
                         #     keeping that write would have left a DEAD WRITER: a config
                         #     file with no reader and a governor that never gets applied.
                         # The applier is therefore ours now: `ceralive-cpu-governor.service`
                         # (mkosi/runtime/, installed by postinst.d/hardware.sh) runs
                         # `cpupower frequency-set -g performance`. `cpufreq-set -g X` maps
                         # to `cpupower frequency-set -g X`; `cpufreq-info` to
                         # `cpupower frequency-info`.
                         #
                         # It is NOT coupled to a Debian kernel despite the 6.12.x version
                         # string: its `Depends:` on arm64 is only `libc6` + `libcpupower1`,
                         # with no `linux-image-*`/`linux-base` dependency, and it drives
                         # the running kernel through sysfs. That is what makes it usable
                         # on the Armbian vendor 6.1 BSP kernel the image actually ships.

# --- Net glue (runtime-critical) ----------------------------------------------
rsync                    # file sync for dev-push/dev-sync live-reload loop (docs/dev-loop.md)
curl                     # http client — CeraUI/update flows + on-device `curl localhost`
                         # health probe (backend observability.ts). Runtime-critical: KEPT.
# wget                   # REMOVED (todo 19, audited). The old "http downloader (update/fetch
                         # infra)" annotation named no consumer, and there is none. The OS
                         # update path is RAUC (`ceralive-update` → `rauc install`) plus
                         # rauc-hawkbit-updater, which downloads through libcurl; the app
                         # update path is `apt-get` against apt.ceralive.tv. CeraUI's backend
                         # cannot spawn it — `wget` is absent from the run.ts `ALLOWED`
                         # allowlist. The ONLY device-side invoker in this repo is
                         # lib/shared/health-gate-lib.sh:54, and it is the fallback arm of
                         # `command -v curl && curl … || wget …`, whose first arm is guaranteed
                         # by `curl` being a mandatory entry above — so the wget arm is
                         # unreachable on any CeraLive image. CeraUI's legacy pre-.deb
                         # install.sh does use wget, but self-provisions it
                         # (`install_if_missing_local wget`) and is superseded by the
                         # ceralive-device package. No installed package Depends/Recommends it.
                         # NOTE: net DIAGNOSTICS (socat/netcat-openbsd/iperf3) moved to the
                         # debug add-on (T17) — verified unused by CeraUI/streaming runtime;
                         # the chaos harness (tools/chaos) ships its own. See removed.md.
sudo                     # required by CeraUI's narrow /etc/sudoers.d/ceralive-addon-helper
                         # drop-in; without the sudo binary add-on enable/disable is dead code.

# --- Console (HDMI readability at boot) ---------------------------------------
kbd                      # setfont/loadkeys. KEPT: ceralive-console-font.service (enabled in
                         # multi-user.target.wants) invokes `setfont` directly, and `setfont`
                         # ships ONLY here. CORRECTION (todo 19, audited): the previous
                         # annotation also claimed systemd-vconsole-setup shells out to these —
                         # that is FALSE for this image. A built rootfs carries NO *vconsole*
                         # file anywhere (no unit, no binary) and no /etc/vconsole.conf, and
                         # console-setup/keyboard configs are absent too, so there is no
                         # vconsole path to break. kbd stays because it is one half of a live,
                         # shipped, enabled unit whose OTHER half — a real PSF provider — is
                         # the tracked gap (size-notes.md §11); dropping kbd would turn that
                         # one-package fix into a two-package one and leave an enabled unit
                         # naming a binary that does not exist.
# fonts-terminus         # REMOVED (todo 31, measured). The annotation this replaces claimed
                         # "large bitmap fonts (ter-v32n etc.) readable at 4K on fbcon" — that is
                         # factually wrong, and re-verified still wrong on trixie (todo 10):
                         # fonts-terminus 1.2.0+ds2-4 ships EXACTLY ONE font file,
                         # /usr/share/fonts/truetype/terminus/TerminusTTF-4.46.0.ttf, and NO
                         # console PSF font at all (the PSF set lives in xfonts-terminus /
                         # console-setup, neither of which is installed). Verified against a built
                         # rootfs: /usr/share/consolefonts/ is EMPTY (0 entries), so
                         # ceralive-console-font.service's
                         #   setfont /usr/share/consolefonts/Lat15-TerminusBold32x16.psf.gz
                         #   || setfont /usr/share/consolefonts/Uni3-TerminusBold32x16.psf.gz || true
                         # can never succeed and silently no-ops on every boot. A TTF has no fbcon
                         # consumer, and the production image ships no X/Wayland; fonts-dejavu-core
                         # (2.88 MB, pulled by fontconfig-config) remains as the only font any
                         # future GUI/kiosk payload would resolve. Restoring HDMI console
                         # readability needs a real PSF provider, NOT this package — tracked
                         # separately, see docs/size-notes.md §11.
                         # NOTE: operational monitoring (htop/iotop/nethogs/vnstat) and the
                         # nano editor moved to the debug add-on (T17) — see removed.md.

# --- OS-update infrastructure (Stage 2; RUNTIME layer, arch-independent) -----
# The device SRT runtime is CeraLive's `libsrt1.5-ceralive` first-party package,
# staged and installed by the App layer. Do not add a Debian `libsrt1.5-*` flavor
# here: GStreamer and cerastream must resolve one forked shared object. The LAN SRT
# ingest listener is served by MediaMTX, so no srt-tools package is needed.
nftables                 # ships /usr/sbin/nft — TWO consumers, one binary.
                         #  (1) ceralive-ingest-firewall.service: DROP inbound :1935 (RTMP) +
                         #      :4001 (SRT) on modem/WWAN/ppp uplink classes so the
                         #      UNAUTHENTICATED v1 MediaMTX ingest gateway is reachable from
                         #      LAN/hotspot ONLY, never from a WAN uplink (DEFERRED.md items
                         #      7 & 8).
                         #  (2) ceralive-share.service (REQ-USB-020..026): the uplink-sharing
                         #      CARRIER. CeraUI renders the desired-state steering/NAT ruleset
                         #      to /run/ceralive/share.nft and the unit validates it with
                         #      `nft -c -f` and applies it with `nft -f`. That table is `inet
                         #      ceralive_share` and is disjoint from the ingest firewall's
                         #      `inet ceralive_ingest_fw` — one binary, two independent tables.
                         # NetworkManager is ALSO pinned to firewall-backend=nftables
                         # (postinst.d/networking.sh), so NM's shared-mode NAT and our
                         # per-uplink masquerade coexist on one backend rather than racing an
                         # iptables shim.
                         # libnftables1 is already pulled by the base (NetworkManager dep), so
                         # the net add is just the nft binary + libmnl/libnftnl (~1.5 MB — see
                         # size-notes.md §7).
conntrack                # ships /usr/sbin/conntrack — the ONLY way to delete conntrack entries
                         # NOTE the name: `conntrack-tools` is the SOURCE package; the BINARY
                         # package Debian trixie ships is `conntrack` (1:1.4.8-2+b1). Asking
                         # for the source name fails the runtime layer with
                         # `E: Unable to locate package conntrack-tools` — re-verified against
                         # the real trixie arm64 index in todo 10, where `conntrack-tools`
                         # STILL resolves to nothing in main/contrib/non-free.
                         # by MARK. On a hard-DOWN uplink transition the steering layer
                         # (CeraUI modules/network/uplink-steering) drains that uplink's flows
                         # in a fixed order: transition ruleset -> MARK-SCOPED `conntrack -D`
                         # -> route-support removal -> final ruleset. Without the flush, every
                         # established client flow stays pinned by its saved ct mark to an
                         # uplink that is already gone and blackholes until it times out;
                         # WITHOUT the `--mark`/`--mask` scoping the same command would tear
                         # down the live SRTLA/engine flows too, which is exactly what the
                         # steering scope invariant forbids. `nft flush` cannot do this — it
                         # removes rules, never conntrack state. Needs CONFIG_NF_CT_NETLINK
                         # (present as a module on the shipped vendor kernel — see
                         # docs/notes/sharing-qdisc-matrix.md).
rauc                     # A/B update client. decisions.md T5. TRIXIE SHIPS 1.13-3+deb13u1
                         # — a five-minor jump from the bookworm 1.8-2 this image used to  # suite-literal-ok: states the previously targeted RAUC version being migrated away from
                         # target, so every "RAUC 1.8 …" statement in this repo was
                         # re-checked in todo 10. The two that MATTER are unchanged:
                         # 1.13 still shells out to `unsquashfs` for a plain-format
                         # bundle's manifest and still to `mkfs.ext4` for an ext4 slot
                         # write — and both are only `Suggests:` of the `rauc` package
                         # (verified in the real trixie index), never `Depends:`, so
                         # `squashfs-tools` and `e2fsprogs` below MUST stay explicit
                         # entries or OTA breaks on-device exactly as it did before.
                         # The `plain` bundle format is also still supported (1.10 only
                         # began WARNING when no format is stated; verity/crypt are not
                         # mandatory), so existing bundles keep installing.
                         # The signing-EKU consequence is real but belongs to todo 12
                         # (RAUC bundle-compat semantics) and is deliberately NOT actioned
                         # here — see mkosi/runtime/rauc/system.conf for the note.
rauc-service             # ships /usr/lib/systemd/system/rauc.service (D-Bus daemon). The
                         # daemon is STILL split out of `rauc` (binary-only) on trixie, and
                         # `rauc-service` is `Architecture: all` there; the runtime postinst
                         # enables rauc.service, so the service pkg remains required.
squashfs-tools           # ships /usr/bin/unsquashfs — rauc shells out to it to extract the
                         # manifest (and rootfs image) from a plain-format .raucb at
                         # `rauc info`/`rauc install` time. Absent it, install fails after
                         # signature verification with "Failed to start unsquashfs: ... No
                         # such file or directory" (real Rock 5B+ hardware). build-time
                         # mksquashfs runs on the HOST/CI, so this runtime gap was invisible.
e2fsprogs                # ships /sbin/mkfs.ext4 — rauc shells out to it to format the target
                         # ext4 slot before copying the new rootfs image during the slot-write
                         # phase of `rauc install`, AFTER signature + manifest checks pass.
                         # Without it, real Rock 5B+ hardware reported exactly: "LastError: Installation error: Failed updating slot rootfs.1: failed to start mkfs.ext4: Failed to execute child process 'mkfs.ext4' (No such file or directory)".
                         # build-time tooling never needed mkfs.ext4, so this runtime-only gap was
                         # invisible; installing e2fsprogs made the REAL end-to-end OTA install
                         # complete successfully (signature verified, slot B activated and rebooted
                         # healthy), proving the fix on physical hardware.
zstd                     # initramfs-tools uses it in the target rootfs to compress initrds instead of falling back to gzip.
                         #   NOTE: this entry alone is too LATE for the build — the runtime layer installs shared.list
                         #   after the platform layer already generated the initrd, so platform/mkosi.postinst installs
                         #   zstd itself ahead of the kernel package. Keep both; this one owns the on-device presence.
u-boot-tools             # fw_setenv/fw_getenv + mkimage — RAUC bootloader env / boot
                         # selector. Generic Debian pkg (present on amd64 too); the
                         # board-specific U-Boot BLOB is a Platform-layer BSP concern.
# rauc-hawkbit-updater   # Stage 7 OTA (task 41). STILL DELIBERATELY a comment, NOT an
                         # active apt line — the trixie migration did NOT change this
                         # verdict, only the suite it is stated against.
                         # VERDICT (todo 10, verified against the real trixie arm64 index
                         # with main+contrib+non-free+non-free-firmware all enabled):
                         # there is NO trixie-native package. `rauc-hawkbit-updater` has
                         # no installation candidate on trixie at all. Debian DOES now
                         # carry it — accepted to unstable 2026-03-16 and migrated to
                         # testing 2026-03-21 as 1.4-1 — but testing is forky, so it
                         # reaches sid/forky and NOT the trixie stable suite this image
                         # targets. Upstream's newest release is still v1.4 (2025-07-14).
                         # So the recipe stays, REBUILT AGAINST TRIXIE: a
                         # 1.4-1~bpo13+1-class backport .deb, pre-staged like the
                         # first-party .debs and installed by the RUNTIME postinst
                         # (setup_hawkbit_updater → apt-get install ./<file>.deb).
                         # ITS RUNTIME DEPS WERE RENAMED BY THE t64 TRANSITION and a
                         # bookworm-era recipe names packages that no longer exist:  # suite-literal-ok: warns against reusing the previously targeted suite's dependency names
                         # `libcurl4` → `libcurl4t64`, `libglib2.0-0` → `libglib2.0-0t64`;
                         # `libjson-glib-1.0-0` KEPT its name. Rebuilding from the current
                         # Debian packaging (salsa `debian/rauc-hawkbit-updater`, 1.4-1)
                         # gets those names right automatically — do not hand-port the old
                         # bookworm dependency list. An active line here would still break  # suite-literal-ok: warns against reusing the previously targeted suite's dependency names
                         # the whole-list `apt-get install` on any host without the backport.
                         # Config/enrollment: mkosi/runtime/hawkbit-updater/. decisions.md Task 5.
